1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:28,090
Chapter 4, embedded development. Because when we go on to develop an operating system, honestly, it involves a lot of techniques related to embedded development. So here, as one chapter, I'll cover some preliminary content for you: I'll introduce the concept of embedded development, the concept of cross-compilation, and some tools and software commonly used in embedded development, including GDB, the QEMU emulator, and MIC.

2
00:00:28,090 --> 00:00:31,910
So first, let's take a look at

3
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:33,150
what embedded development is.

4
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:39,040
So, embedded development,

5
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,680
you can look at this diagram.

6
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:42,720
This diagram is actually a

7
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,060
if later on you go out and do this kind of

8
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:47,820
say, developing drivers,

9
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:49,600
developing operating systems,

10
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:50,900
that kind of work,

11
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,180
then this is actually the kind of work environment you'll have.

12
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:54,500
It's a bit different from our

13
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:56,320
non-embedded development,

14
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,780
that is, the common local development environment.

15
00:00:58,780 --> 00:00:59,520
It's a little different,

16
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:00,800
because if it's local development,

17
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:04,460
basically just having one computer like this is enough.

18
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:04,960
Right?

19
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,500
For example, you just grab a computer here,

20
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:08,140
and as long as it can connect to the internet,

21
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:10,940
Basically, your environment is pretty much all set up.

22
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:14,120
But if you're doing embedded development,

23
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:16,060
that is far from enough.

24
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:17,880
In particular, we...

25
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,100
because the programs we need to run

26
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:21,240
often have to run on

27
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:22,640
a separate

28
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:24,620
what we call a target board.

29
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:25,440
That is, on

30
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:26,440
say, a development board,

31
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:27,980
or on another machine.

32
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:29,660
In that case,

33
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:32,720
we at least need to set up a small development environment,

34
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:34,560
maybe through a router,

35
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:37,400
connecting those two machines together.

36
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:38,440
Now, this machine here,

37
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:39,600
is called the target board,

38
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,140
and this machine here is called the host,

39
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:42,320
meaning the main machine,

40
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:43,420
and this one is called the target machine.

41
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:45,100
on the host machine, generally,

42
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:49,420
we do things like compiling, editing, and debugging.

43
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,800
And this target board,

44
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,140
what mainly runs on it

45
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:54,820
might be running on another machine,

46
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:55,740
right?

47
00:01:55,740 --> 00:01:58,300
Just get familiar with this setup.

48
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:01,800
Now, embedded development,

49
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,560
it happens in a specific hardware environment,

50
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:06,540
developing for a particular piece of hardware.

51
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:08,680
So the connection with the hardware

52
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:10,620
is quite tight.

53
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:11,360
Very often you need to

54
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:13,360
keep in mind that your program

55
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:16,060
is not running locally,

56
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:18,660
but on some special hardware.

57
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:20,360
What exactly is on that hardware,

58
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:21,460
you need to be quite familiar with it,

59
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:24,200
and only then can you do the development.

60
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:25,740
Well, with an operating system, that's even more true.

61
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:26,800
Because when you develop an operating system,

62
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:27,840
you definitely need to know

63
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:30,640
the machine your operating system runs on

64
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:31,660
is

65
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:33,020
for example,

66
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:35,700
whether the CPU is x86,

67
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:37,020
or RISC-V,

68
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:37,320
right?

69
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:38,480
So at the very least, that's what you need to know.

70
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,020
And you can't really develop it locally,

71
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:40,740
because with local development,

72
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:42,040
there's already an operating system on your local machine.

73
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:43,560
So of course there's no need for you to develop another operating system.

74
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:44,860
Your operating system is definitely going to run on a

75
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:47,320
machine that has no system on it,

76
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:48,140
right?

77
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:49,160
So,

78
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:52,000
if you want to build an operating system,

79
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,660
it actually falls under a kind of lightweight agile development,

80
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:58,090
and so

81
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:01,090
So, since this is embedded development,

82
00:03:01,090 --> 00:03:04,010
meaning the target machine and the host are two separate machines,

83
00:03:04,010 --> 00:03:06,210
we came up with a concept,

84
00:03:06,210 --> 00:03:08,110
called the concept of cross-compilation.

85
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:12,110
Now, what exactly is this concept?

86
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:14,210
If you haven't done embedded development before,

87
00:03:14,210 --> 00:03:14,890
you probably know very little about it.

88
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:17,810
So I'll go ahead and explain it to everyone here.

89
00:03:17,810 --> 00:03:19,310
First, let's say

90
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:21,450
we can define a few concepts:

91
00:03:21,450 --> 00:03:22,650
the build system,

92
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:25,090
the host system, and the target system.

93
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:27,490
I've already circled the important parts here

94
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:28,870
in black text,

95
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:30,590
so feel free to follow along with me.

96
00:03:30,590 --> 00:03:32,030
What is the build system?

97
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:32,650
The build system.

98
00:03:32,650 --> 00:03:33,950
What is it used for?

99
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:37,470
It's used to generate the compiler and other auxiliary programs that let you control compilation.

100
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:39,910
That is to say, the GCC you normally use.

101
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:40,730
This kind of compiler

102
00:03:40,730 --> 00:03:42,370
actually has to be built as well

103
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:42,910
think about it, right?

104
00:03:42,910 --> 00:03:44,770
It's not just there by nature

105
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:46,110
It has to be built too

106
00:03:46,110 --> 00:03:47,130
So how do we build it?

107
00:03:47,130 --> 00:03:49,390
On which machine do we build it?

108
00:03:49,390 --> 00:03:52,090
That machine is called the build system

109
00:03:52,090 --> 00:03:54,910
And once it's built, what do we do with it?

110
00:03:54,910 --> 00:03:55,970
We run it, right?

111
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:56,610
Exactly

112
00:03:56,610 --> 00:03:59,630
So on which machine do we run this compiler?

113
00:03:59,630 --> 00:04:02,130
The system where this compiler runs

114
00:04:02,130 --> 00:04:03,530
is called the host system

115
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:07,170
So what is the compiler for?

116
00:04:07,170 --> 00:04:10,550
The compiler is used to generate another program

117
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:11,050
think about it, right?

118
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:12,030
It is itself a program

119
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:14,010
and it's used to generate another program

120
00:04:14,010 --> 00:04:15,830
And the application program it generates

121
00:04:15,830 --> 00:04:17,230
Where does it run? It runs on the target machine.

122
00:04:17,230 --> 00:04:18,610
So the machine that runs this program is called the target system.

123
00:04:18,610 --> 00:04:21,770
So we have three concepts:

124
00:04:21,770 --> 00:04:23,090
the build system, the host system, and the target system.

125
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:24,690
With these three concepts,

126
00:04:24,690 --> 00:04:28,370
we can define what native compilation is,

127
00:04:28,370 --> 00:04:30,490
and what cross-compilation is.

128
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:32,630
The formula is very simple.

129
00:04:32,630 --> 00:04:34,010
You should be able to read it yourselves.

130
00:04:34,010 --> 00:04:36,030
What does build equals host equals target mean?

131
00:04:36,030 --> 00:04:37,770
Let me translate it into plain language.

132
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:41,090
What does it mean?

133
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:43,170
It means that,

134
00:04:43,170 --> 00:04:44,310
for example, on this computer of mine,

135
00:04:44,310 --> 00:04:45,570
I built my compiler on this computer,

136
00:04:45,570 --> 00:04:48,110
right?

137
00:04:48,110 --> 00:04:50,650
And at the same time,

138
00:04:50,650 --> 00:04:53,190
the compiler itself also runs on this computer,

139
00:04:53,190 --> 00:04:54,050
and the programs it produces also run on this computer.

140
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:55,190
In that case, this is called native compilation.

141
00:04:55,190 --> 00:04:58,330
I'm also running my compiler on my computer,

142
00:04:58,330 --> 00:04:59,850
and at the same time,

143
00:04:59,850 --> 00:05:01,310
what I generate —

144
00:05:01,310 --> 00:05:03,170
the application programs I build with my compiler —

145
00:05:03,170 --> 00:05:04,470
also run on the same computer I'm on.

146
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:07,910
So what do we call this way of working?

147
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:08,970
It's called native compilation.

148
00:05:08,970 --> 00:05:12,570
If you're not involved in embedded development,

149
00:05:12,570 --> 00:05:14,490
most of the work you do is actually native compilation.

150
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:17,350
It's just that you probably don't build the compiler yourselves —

151
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:20,070
you probably just grab a compiler,

152
00:05:20,070 --> 00:05:20,270
right?

153
00:05:20,270 --> 00:05:22,130
But your computer —

154
00:05:22,130 --> 00:05:23,670
say your computer is x86 —

155
00:05:23,670 --> 00:05:26,070
then that compiler you grabbed,

156
00:05:26,070 --> 00:05:26,950
it was probably also built on a machine that —

157
00:05:26,950 --> 00:05:30,210
most likely, it was also compiled on an X86 machine.

158
00:05:30,210 --> 00:05:32,270
So your build system and your host system,

159
00:05:32,270 --> 00:05:33,150
they're the same system.

160
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:34,090
And at the same time,

161
00:05:34,090 --> 00:05:36,490
The executable program you generate with this compiler,

162
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:37,990
doesn't it also run on x86?

163
00:05:37,990 --> 00:05:39,090
So naturally,

164
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:40,630
this way of working,

165
00:05:40,630 --> 00:05:42,990
is what we call native compilation.

166
00:05:42,990 --> 00:05:45,270
As for cross-compilation,

167
00:05:45,270 --> 00:05:46,510
in general,

168
00:05:46,510 --> 00:05:47,510
the way we define cross-compilation,

169
00:05:47,510 --> 00:05:49,150
is that the first two are the same,

170
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:51,410
but mainly, the executable you generate,

171
00:05:51,410 --> 00:05:52,970
does not run on your host machine.

172
00:05:52,970 --> 00:05:54,710
Just like in that diagram earlier,

173
00:05:54,710 --> 00:05:59,290
you generate an executable here,

174
00:05:59,290 --> 00:06:00,170
let's say it's called A.out,

175
00:06:00,170 --> 00:06:02,490
but this A.out,

176
00:06:02,490 --> 00:06:03,990
does it run on this computer?

177
00:06:03,990 --> 00:06:04,930
No, it doesn't.

178
00:06:04,930 --> 00:06:06,990
You have to use a network or some other method,

179
00:06:06,990 --> 00:06:08,810
to take this A.out application and do what with it?

180
00:06:08,810 --> 00:06:11,930
You have to download it onto this board to run it.

181
00:06:11,930 --> 00:06:13,050
You also understand what I'm saying

182
00:06:13,050 --> 00:06:15,650
the processor on this board

183
00:06:15,650 --> 00:06:16,890
and the processor in your computer

184
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:19,090
might not even be the same instruction set at all

185
00:06:19,090 --> 00:06:21,190
Your machine might be X86

186
00:06:21,190 --> 00:06:24,710
while this one on top might be RISC 5

187
00:06:24,710 --> 00:06:29,470
In that kind of situation, you get that cross

188
00:06:29,470 --> 00:06:31,410
compilation working environment

189
00:06:31,410 --> 00:06:32,930
Everyone can follow, right?

190
00:06:32,930 --> 00:06:42,750
We also have a diagram here

191
00:06:42,750 --> 00:06:43,470
that everyone can take a look at

192
00:06:43,470 --> 00:06:46,220
Let's take another look

193
00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:48,140
This diagram is actually also talking about this same thing

194
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:51,740
That is, combining the formula we just talked about

195
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:53,260
If, for example, look at this

196
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:55,780
This tool of ours is called...

197
00:06:55,780 --> 00:06:57,580
We normally type GCC

198
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:00,620
But actually, when we type GCC

199
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:04,620
this GCC is really just a soft link

200
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:10,350
I don't know if any of you have ever looked into it

201
00:07:10,350 --> 00:07:12,970
So where is this GCC of yours?

202
00:07:12,970 --> 00:07:14,710
For example, when we type gcc,

203
00:07:14,710 --> 00:07:16,310
what exactly is this gcc?

204
00:07:16,310 --> 00:07:20,180
We can type "whereis gcc",

205
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:22,700
and as you can see, after we run whereis gcc,

206
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:23,460
it tells us

207
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:25,420
that your gcc is actually at this full path right here.

208
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:26,120
Right?

209
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:27,920
So what exactly is this thing?

210
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:30,360
Let's dig a little deeper.

211
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:40,460
You can see that the gcc you're running

212
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:41,520
is actually this thing.

213
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:42,800
Now, is this thing

214
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:44,020
actually an application program?

215
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:44,760
Not quite.

216
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:45,700
What is it actually?

217
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:47,860
It's a symbolic link.

218
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:48,520
Do you all see that?

219
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:50,060
The concept of a symbolic link.

220
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:50,620
If you're not familiar with it,

221
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:51,980
Read this one yourself.

222
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:52,940
So this

223
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:54,220
it points to that again.

224
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:55,420
Now what is this thing again?

225
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:56,660
Let's keep tracing.

226
00:07:56,660 --> 00:08:03,940
This one is still a symbolic link.

227
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:04,720
Layer after layer of wrapping.

228
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:05,940
It actually wraps it up.

229
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:07,860
This is for everyone's convenience.

230
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:09,160
It actually points to this.

231
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:10,480
Now this thing,

232
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:11,700
if we trace it further,

233
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:20,800
I didn't type that wrong, did I?

234
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:23,620
OK

235
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:24,700
At this point we've already...

236
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:26,460
ZR8 is no longer a symbolic link.

237
00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:28,740
This is the thing we actually run.

238
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:29,520
In other words, when you type,

239
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:30,520
when you type GCC,

240
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:32,020
what you're actually running is this thing.

241
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:34,500
This actually tells us

242
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:36,540
it's actually a build tool that runs

243
00:08:36,540 --> 00:08:40,040
on an x86 64-bit machine

244
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:41,120
a compiler

245
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:43,680
So that means,

246
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:46,100
your compiler actually

247
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:47,760
runs on x86

248
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:48,860
At the same time,

249
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:49,140
we

250
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:50,440
but in order to

251
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:52,160
in order to compile our program,

252
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:53,960
what do we need to do first?

253
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:55,540
We need to build a compiler ourselves,

254
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:57,240
because with this program,

255
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:00,200
when we normally type GCC,

256
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:01,680
the a.out you compile

257
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:03,560
can only run on the x86 machine

258
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:04,620
You can't run it on

259
00:09:04,620 --> 00:09:06,320
a RISC-V machine

260
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:07,460
You all know that, right?

261
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:09,640
So if we just use GCC directly,

262
00:09:09,640 --> 00:09:10,720
it won't work.

263
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:13,100
We have to build a compiler ourselves.

264
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:15,900
Of course, the process of building that compiler

265
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:17,160
is something I won't go into here.

266
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:17,860
This compiler,

267
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:19,440
we've already built it for you,

268
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:20,380
and placed it up front,

269
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:21,240
in that tools directory

270
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:22,660
that I introduced to you earlier.

271
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:23,860
So this tool

272
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:26,200
is made using that compiler,

273
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:28,340
because once we have the source code,

274
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:29,680
we can use that compiler

275
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:31,020
to produce

276
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:34,180
a compiler for RISC-V 64-bit machines.

277
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:36,900
Then when we do our daily work,

278
00:09:36,900 --> 00:09:40,300
we use this compiler for development.

279
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:42,240
